WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
For years, rendering a UI in React Native meant passing instructions across an asynchronous

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.600
communication bridge, a structure that often bottlenecked under rabid, complex updates.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.280
Previously, the JavaScript thread communicated with native views by routing instructions

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:25.040
through a central bottleneck known as the UI manager. In 0.76, the legacy UI manager is completely

5
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:31.120
removed. It is replaced by fabric components, establishing a direct, unified connection between

6
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.760
JavaScript and the native layer. A fabric component is, at its core, a native operating system view

7
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.040
that is exposed directly to React. Unlike the old asynchronous bridge, where JavaScript

8
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.560
dispatched an update and waited for a delayed render, this architecture allows React to measure and

9
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.560
manipulate native views synchronously. The UI renders without the previous lag. Building UI

10
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.280
under this rendering engine requires developers to adhere to a strict, standardized set of code

11
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.280
artifacts. To understand this system, we will construct a fabric component, trace its live event

12
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:13.520
flow, and examine legacy code migration. In version 0.76, the fabric C++ contract becomes the native

13
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.720
standard for every view, whether it is written as a new component or wrapped for backward compatibility.

14
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.840
This grid represents your project directory. Constructing any new fabric component

15
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:30.640
requires exactly four distinct files. Step 1 is defining the components API. You write a strict

16
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.320
TypeScript specification file, detailing exactly what properties and events the view will accept.

17
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.360
This specification acts as the single source of truth for your component. Nothing can be passed to

18
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.560
the native view unless it is explicitly declared here first. This strictness exists to feed the

19
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:52.160
automated engine at the center of the architecture. Code Gen. During the build phase, Code Gen reads your

20
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.080
TypeScript specification. It parses your JavaScript types and translates them into C++ fabric

21
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.320
bindings, generating the second required artifact without any manual intervention. These C++ bindings

22
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.360
ensure strict type safety across the boundary between JavaScript and the native operating system.

23
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.680
If a property type mismatches, the build fails before a runtime crash can ever occur.

24
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.120
Steps 3 and 4 are the implementation. You write the actual platform-specific code,

25
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.920
an iOS native view, and an Android native view. These native views are programmed to snap

26
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.840
directly into the auto-generated C++ interfaces, satisfying the contract established by Code Gen.

27
00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.640
Previously, developers manually passed weekly type to JSON objects over the bridge, hoping the

28
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.840
native side parsed them correctly. Now, this four-part pipeline forces both JavaScript and native code

29
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.760
to adhere to a strict C++ contract, eliminating the entire class of payload serialization errors.

30
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.760
With the static files compiled, we can observe exactly how this C++ contract behaves when a

31
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.640
component is running live on a device. This split screen shows the official Expo Snack Slider environment.

32
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.640
On the left is a physical device displaying a UI slider. On the right is the React Dev Tools Inspector.

33
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.920
We use Expo Go here because it is configured to route native components through the fabric

34
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.600
bridge by default. To monitor the internal data, you physically shake the test device to trigger

35
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.600
the developer menu and open the inspector panel. Watch the system respond as a user drags the slider

36
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.280
back and forth on the screen. The physical interaction on the screen immediately generates a native

37
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.880
data packet containing the new positional coordinates. This payload instantly traverses the new

38
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.040
synchronous fabric bridge. It arrives at a C++ entity known as the shadow node, which sits in memory

39
00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.800
managing the components layout and state. The shadow node processes the coordinates, updates its

40
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.160
internal state, and pushes the new property values directly up to the JavaScript thread.

41
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.560
On the right side of the screen, you see those exact coordinates arrive in Dev Tools,

42
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.680
synchronized perfectly with the user's thumb movement. Because physical interactions now update

43
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.800
the shadow node via C++, state changes are deterministic. The UI responds instantly, entirely bypassing

44
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.080
the unpredictable latency of the old rendering bridge. This introduces an immediate problem.

45
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.600
If the UI manager is gone, why don't the thousands of existing third-party react native libraries

46
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:24.640
instantly crash in 0.76? The solution shipped in 0.76 is a backward compatibility system called

47
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.400
the interop layer. Mechanically, the interop layer intercepts and wraps around legacy UI manager-based

48
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:36.000
view creation calls. It acts as a fabric compatible shell, dynamically translating old asynchronous

49
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.960
commands into a format the modern synchronous rendering pipeline understands. For developers,

50
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.560
the primary advantage of this layer is its transparency to JavaScript. You do not need to rewrite your

51
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.920
code or change a single import statement to activate this backward compatibility. By default,

52
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.680
this automatic translation successfully covers approximately 95% of the community libraries currently

53
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.760
in use. The interop layer serves as a critical safety net. It prevents a fractured ecosystem,

54
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.680
allowing teams to upgrade their core architecture to 0.76 today while delaying extensive component

55
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.280
rewrites. This leaves developers operating in a dual reality. New components use the strict

56
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.200
TypeScript to code Gen pipeline, though existing dependencies operate safely inside the interop shell.

57
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.560
However, this leaves the remaining 5%, the edge cases where the interop layer cannot provide a

58
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.440
seamless translation. The first technical trigger that breaks compatibility is a library's heavy

59
00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.600
reliance on the set native props method to directly manipulate views. The second trigger is a deep,

60
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.680
complex integration with internal UI manager logic that cannot be safely abstracted. For these

61
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.360
specific edge cases, the interop layer will fail. Components relying on these patterns will crash

62
00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.840
in 0.76. The only resolution for maintainers of these complex libraries is to manually rewrite their

63
00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.280
rendering logic from scratch. That requires returning to step 1, defining a strict TypeScript

64
00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:07.600
specification, and letting code Gen generate the bindings. React Native 0.76 provides a safety net for

65
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.720
the vast majority of applications, but accessing the full performance benefits of the architecture

66
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.760
ultimately requires fully embracing the Fabric C++ contract.

